-- Q880: SOC Engagements [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Management''s written assertion about controls","The auditor''s independent opinion on controls","The user entity''s representation of reliance","The regulatory compliance certificate for controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320), management of the service organization provides a written assertion that its system description is fairly presented and that controls were suitably designed and operated effectively. The service auditor examines this assertion and issues an independent opinion.
Wrong (B): The auditor''s opinion is the practitioner''s independent evaluation of management''s assertion, not the assertion itself.
Wrong (C): User entities may provide representations, but the SOC 1 assertion is made by the service organization''s management.
Wrong (D): No regulatory compliance certificate is part of the SOC 1 reporting framework.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 880;

-- Q881: SOC Engagements [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The subservice organization must always be excluded from scope","The inclusive method or the carve-out method may be used","Subservice organizations are addressed only in SOC 1 reports","The user entity must independently audit the subservice organization"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AT-C Section 320 and the AICPA Guide: SOC 2 Reporting, subservice organizations are addressed through the inclusive method (included in scope and tested) or the carve-out method (excluded with a description of services and expected controls). Both approaches are valid under SSAE 18.
Wrong (A): Exclusion is not mandatory; the inclusive method allows the subservice organization to be included in scope.
Wrong (C): Subservice organizations are relevant in both SOC 1 and SOC 2 engagements.
Wrong (D): The user entity may need separate assurance, but auditing the subservice is not their responsibility under the SOC framework.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 881;

-- Q884: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Availability (uptime commitments)","Confidentiality (data protection)","Security (Common Criteria)","Privacy (personal information)"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Security, also known as the Common Criteria, is the foundational category required in every SOC 2 engagement per the AICPA Trust Services Criteria (TSP Section 100). It addresses protection of information and systems against unauthorized access, disclosure, and damage.
Wrong (A): Availability is an optional criterion addressing system uptime and operational commitments.
Wrong (B): Confidentiality is an optional criterion focused on protecting designated confidential information.
Wrong (D): Privacy is an optional criterion covering personal information handling practices.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 884;

-- Q885: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Security (unauthorized access protection)","Availability (uptime and usability)","Confidentiality (designated data protection)","Processing Integrity (accuracy and completeness)"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Processing Integrity addresses whether system processing is complete, valid, accurate, timely, and authorized per AICPA TSP Section 100. It ensures that transactions are processed correctly and outputs are reliable.
Wrong (A): Security addresses protection against unauthorized access and disclosure, not processing accuracy.
Wrong (B): Availability addresses whether the system is operational and usable as committed, not output correctness.
Wrong (C): Confidentiality addresses protection of designated confidential information, not processing completeness.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 885;

-- Q886: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
