-- Q890: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the report as sufficient since Security implies Processing Integrity","Request expanded SOC 2 scope or perform independent audit procedures","Issue a qualified opinion on the user entity''s financial statements","Ignore the gap since SOC 2 reports are voluntary engagements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AICPA TSP Section 100, each SOC 2 criterion addresses distinct control objectives. Coverage of Security and Availability does not extend to Processing Integrity. The user auditor should request the service organization include Processing Integrity in its next report or perform independent testing.
Wrong (A): Security and Availability criteria do not imply or substitute for Processing Integrity, which evaluates separate control objectives.
Wrong (C): A gap in the SOC 2 report scope does not automatically require a qualified opinion on the user entity''s financial statements.
Wrong (D): The voluntary nature of SOC 2 does not relieve the auditor of obtaining needed assurance over relevant controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 890;

-- Q894: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate the qualification''s impact and test compensating controls","Disregard it since the qualification covers only four months","Automatically issue a qualified opinion on the user entity''s statements","Request the service organization reissue the report without qualification"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AU-C Section 402, the user auditor must evaluate the significance of the qualification, assess the nature and duration of the control failure, and determine whether compensating controls mitigate the risk. Expanded substantive procedures may be needed for the affected four-month period.
Wrong (B): A four-month control failure is significant and cannot be disregarded simply because it covers less than the full period.
Wrong (C): A SOC 2 qualification does not automatically result in a qualified opinion for the user entity; the auditor must exercise professional judgment.
Wrong (D): Requesting removal of a legitimate qualification is inappropriate and would undermine the integrity of the report.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 894;

-- Q895: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It uses fundamentally different attestation standards than SOC 2","It examines entity-wide cybersecurity risk management, not one system","It is available exclusively to government agencies and contractors","It does not require a CPA firm to perform the examination"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AICPA SOC for Cybersecurity guidance (AT-C Section 205), this framework examines the entire organization''s cybersecurity risk management program, whereas SOC 2 focuses on controls for a specific system or service. It uses AICPA description criteria rather than Trust Services Criteria.
Wrong (A): Both SOC 2 and SOC for Cybersecurity use attestation standards within the SSAE framework; they are not fundamentally different.
Wrong (C): SOC for Cybersecurity is available to any organization, not restricted to government agencies.
Wrong (D): A CPA firm is required to perform SOC for Cybersecurity examinations, just as with SOC 2.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 895;

-- Q1050: IT General Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Approve both for simultaneous deployment to minimize window duration","Evaluate combined risk and sequence or defer one change accordingly","Reject both since two changes in one window always violate policy","Delegate the decision to the database administrator for technical review"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ITIL 4 Section 5.2 (Change Enablement), the CAB evaluates proposed changes for risk, impact, and resource requirements. When multiple changes affect the same environment, the CAB must assess combined risk and potential interactions, then sequence with rollback checkpoints or defer one to a separate window.
Wrong (A): Simultaneous deployment ignores interaction risk between changes affecting the same claims processing environment.
Wrong (C): Multiple changes in one window are not automatically prohibited; the CAB evaluates each situation based on risk.
Wrong (D): The CAB serves as the governance and authorization body; delegation to a single administrator bypasses the advisory process.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1050;

-- Q1213: SOC Testing Controls [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A deviation is a control not operating as described; an exception is a deviation deemed a control failure","A deviation is a minor error while an exception is a major error","There is no distinction because the terms are interchangeable","A deviation applies to Type I reports while an exception applies to Type II"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AICPA Guide: SOC 2 Reporting Section 4, a deviation is any instance where a control did not operate as described for a specific test item. An exception is a deviation the practitioner concludes represents a failure to meet the control objective. Not every deviation rises to exception status.
Wrong (B): The distinction is not based on severity (minor vs. major) but on whether the practitioner concludes the deviation represents a control failure.
Wrong (C): The terms have distinct technical meanings in SOC reporting and are not interchangeable.
Wrong (D): Both terms apply to Type II engagements where operating effectiveness is tested; the distinction is not report-type based.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1213;

-- Q1214: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether the pass rate alone determines control effectiveness","Whether the opinion must be automatically qualified for any deviation","Whether the sample should always be doubled when deviations occur","Whether it reflects a design or operating effectiveness issue with compensating controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): When deviations are found, the auditor must evaluate whether the deviation reflects a control design problem or an operating effectiveness failure, and whether compensating controls mitigate the risk per AICPA SOC 2 guidance. This assessment drives the auditor''s response.
Wrong (A): Pass rates must be considered alongside the nature and cause of deviations, not used as the sole determinant of effectiveness.
Wrong (B): Not all deviations automatically require qualification; the auditor exercises professional judgment about significance.
Wrong (C): Sample expansion is one possible response but is not automatic or always required when deviations are found.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1214;

