-- Q3457: IT Infrastructure and Architecture [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["56-bit DES keys are vulnerable to brute-force attacks with modern hardware","DES encryption processes data too quickly for modern network protocols","VPN concentrators should operate without any encryption algorithms","DES is outdated only for government systems, not commercial applications"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-175B, DES with 56-bit keys is deprecated because modern computing power makes brute-force attacks feasible. NIST requires a minimum of AES-128 or AES-256 for protecting sensitive data in transit. Financial data transmitted using DES is vulnerable to interception and decryption.
Wrong (B): DES''s processing speed is not the security concern; the issue is the insufficient key length allowing brute-force attacks.
Wrong (C): VPN concentrators require encryption to protect data in transit; operating without encryption would be a critical vulnerability.
Wrong (D): NIST deprecation of DES applies to all uses, both government and commercial, due to the fundamental weakness of 56-bit keys.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3457;

